WEBVTT

1
00:00:01,876 --> 00:00:04,129
[♪ classical music playing]

2
00:00:05,672 --> 00:00:10,677
{\an8}The drummer is, in my opinion,
the center of the universe.

3
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:14,472
[♪ chorus vocalizing]

4
00:00:17,684 --> 00:00:22,355
{\an8}Nobody's really boppin'
to the vocals or the guitar.

5
00:00:22,731 --> 00:00:24,691
{\an8}As a drummer, you are moving people.

6
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:29,738
{\an8}[Lil Wayne] <i>Drums are not</i>
<i>too complicated to feel,</i>

7
00:00:29,821 --> 00:00:33,366
{\an8}and when you feel music,
that's unforgettable.

8
00:00:35,744 --> 00:00:38,663
[Stewart Copeland]
<i>All of the other extrapolations, melody</i>

9
00:00:38,747 --> 00:00:42,292
{\an8}are just decoration
for the fundamental business of music,

10
00:00:42,375 --> 00:00:44,544
{\an8}which is to motivate the pelvic region

11
00:00:44,627 --> 00:00:49,132
<i>and induce humans involuntarily</i>
<i>to perform a mating dance,</i>

12
00:00:49,215 --> 00:00:52,052
in public, driven by the drums.

13
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:57,766
[Stewart laughs]

14
00:00:57,849 --> 00:00:59,225
Those fucking guitarists!

15
00:00:59,309 --> 00:01:00,602
Wha-- Who needs those guys?

16
00:01:00,685 --> 00:01:02,395
It's the beat they're dancing to.

17
00:01:02,479 --> 00:01:04,731
[♪ dramatic music playing]

18
00:01:04,814 --> 00:01:08,818
{\an8}Travis has a very unique style,
instantly identifiable.

19
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:13,531
<i>You know, the physicality</i>
<i>is just mind-boggling.</i>

20
00:01:16,618 --> 00:01:18,453
[Taylor Hawkins]
<i>Everyone's got their own feel, you know?</i>

21
00:01:18,536 --> 00:01:21,790
{\an8}I mean, some bands have drummers
that make them cook

22
00:01:21,873 --> 00:01:23,500
{\an8}and put 'em on the edge all the time.

23
00:01:25,919 --> 00:01:28,713
{\an8}When I see Travis playing the drums,
it really looks like

24
00:01:28,797 --> 00:01:30,173
{\an8}he's beating the shit out of somebody.

25
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:33,635
♪♪

26
00:01:37,347 --> 00:01:40,683
{\an8}Aggression, pain, anger,
he's been through all that.

27
00:01:44,729 --> 00:01:46,981
[Matt Skiba] <i>You know, the things that</i>
<i>he's lived through and survived,</i>

28
00:01:47,065 --> 00:01:50,235
<i>I think that most people</i>
<i>would just curl up into a ball.</i>

29
00:01:53,029 --> 00:01:56,533
{\an8}But I think that there is
a higher force at play.

30
00:01:56,616 --> 00:01:58,827
♪♪

31
00:02:00,954 --> 00:02:03,289
[MGK] <i>Falling from the sky like Icarus...</i>

32
00:02:03,957 --> 00:02:08,002
{\an8}probably does some crazy shit
to your head.

33
00:02:10,088 --> 00:02:14,467
[YUNGBLUD] <i>Every single hit</i>
<i>is him communicating</i>

34
00:02:14,551 --> 00:02:16,094
<i>his story to the world.</i>

35
00:02:17,512 --> 00:02:19,305
<i>And, dude, he has got a crazy story.</i>

36
00:02:20,598 --> 00:02:22,183
{\an8}Fuck me, he's got a crazy story.

37
00:02:22,725 --> 00:02:25,687
♪♪

38
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,692
[faint siren wailing]

39
00:02:34,529 --> 00:02:37,240
[airplane whooshing]

40
00:02:37,323 --> 00:02:39,742
[explosion booms]

41
00:02:43,204 --> 00:02:45,999
[sirens wailing]

42
00:02:46,082 --> 00:02:49,294
[reporter] <i>The witness told us he was</i>
<i>a few hundred yards away last night</i>

43
00:02:49,377 --> 00:02:53,339
<i>and saw what he called</i>
<i>"a fireball rolling across the highway."</i>

44
00:02:53,423 --> 00:02:58,761
<i>Then he said he saw two men</i>
<i>who were standing in the road on fire</i>

45
00:02:58,845 --> 00:03:02,473
<i>on fire hitting each other</i>
<i>trying to put the fire out.</i>

46
00:03:10,481 --> 00:03:13,359
[groans, yells]

47
00:03:13,443 --> 00:03:15,778
[fire roaring]

48
00:03:26,873 --> 00:03:29,459
[waves crashing]

49
00:03:30,418 --> 00:03:32,503
[drums thudding]

50
00:03:39,844 --> 00:03:41,679
[cymbals crashing]

51
00:03:48,645 --> 00:03:52,023
[Travis Barker] <i>Since the crash,</i>
<i>I've never stepped foot on a plane.</i>

52
00:03:54,275 --> 00:03:55,944
I can't even fathom the thought.

53
00:03:56,027 --> 00:03:59,113
And-And when I do even put it
in my head and I let it--

54
00:03:59,197 --> 00:04:01,282
I let my mind even think about it...

55
00:04:03,159 --> 00:04:05,828
I have the worst flashbacks
of the accident,

56
00:04:05,912 --> 00:04:07,538
and I just, I can't do it.

57
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:13,086
<i>I want to stay as far away</i>
<i>from death or anything</i>

58
00:04:13,169 --> 00:04:15,004
<i>that could bring death my way...</i>

59
00:04:16,005 --> 00:04:19,008
<i>in any way possible.</i>

60
00:04:19,509 --> 00:04:22,387
[♪ gentle music playing]

61
00:04:23,054 --> 00:04:25,556
<i>I take the </i>Queen Mary<i> to Europe.</i>

62
00:04:26,557 --> 00:04:30,728
<i>It's a nine-day cruise</i>
<i>straight through the Atlantic.</i>

63
00:04:32,689 --> 00:04:34,148
<i>It's really the only way I can tour.</i>

64
00:04:37,110 --> 00:04:39,946
[Lawrence Vavra] <i>We don't wanna have</i>
<i>Travis doing anything unsafe.</i>

65
00:04:40,989 --> 00:04:43,324
<i>But it's not the ideal touring scenario.</i>

66
00:04:45,618 --> 00:04:48,413
{\an8}His band mates, they all understand.

67
00:04:48,496 --> 00:04:50,456
{\an8}Everyone gets it, you know?

68
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:53,459
♪♪

69
00:04:53,543 --> 00:04:55,712
<i>He knows the route so well now,</i>

70
00:04:55,795 --> 00:05:00,049
<i>that he knows exactly</i>
<i>when they're around the </i>Titanic.

71
00:05:02,093 --> 00:05:04,137
<i>You know, when those waters get high...</i>

72
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:05,513
[waves crashing]

73
00:05:05,596 --> 00:05:07,473
<i>you know, he stays</i>
<i>with his life jacket sometimes.</i>

74
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:11,561
<i>He's got this fear, you know,</i>

75
00:05:11,644 --> 00:05:14,981
<i>that, at any time,</i>
<i>he's not gonna be on this earth.</i>

76
00:05:19,694 --> 00:05:20,903
[Dr. John Briere]
<i>When you've been traumatized,</i>

77
00:05:20,987 --> 00:05:22,697
<i>your physiology shifts in some ways,</i>

78
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:24,991
<i>probably from an evolutionary perspective.</i>

79
00:05:26,909 --> 00:05:29,495
{\an8}Your memories tend to become
what we call hyper-consolidated,

80
00:05:29,579 --> 00:05:32,832
{\an8}which means that they get
very strongly ingrained in the mind

81
00:05:32,915 --> 00:05:34,542
{\an8}in a very vivid sort of way.

82
00:05:37,086 --> 00:05:39,297
<i>But for some of us,</i>
<i>if the trauma is severe enough,</i>

83
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:40,923
<i>we may think that we're back in the event</i>

84
00:05:41,007 --> 00:05:43,009
<i>or that the event's happening</i>
<i>all over again.</i>

85
00:05:44,761 --> 00:05:46,637
[indistinct radio chatter]

86
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:48,389
[Travis]
<i>Get us an ambulance, man.</i>

87
00:05:48,473 --> 00:05:50,391
[Travis groans, yells]

88
00:05:53,895 --> 00:05:56,564
[Travis] <i>I've taken the </i>Queen Mary,
<i>I think, six times now.</i>

89
00:05:58,524 --> 00:05:59,692
<i>I've gotten used to it,</i>

90
00:05:59,776 --> 00:06:01,986
<i>and I've really figured out</i>
<i>my rhythm on there.</i>

91
00:06:03,613 --> 00:06:07,533
<i>I just focus on training</i>
<i>and playing drums for hours a day.</i>

92
00:06:10,912 --> 00:06:13,915
The first time I came on the boat,
I brought a drum kit,

93
00:06:13,998 --> 00:06:17,919
and I would set up in a room
they provided for me.

94
00:06:19,087 --> 00:06:21,547
<i>Sometimes I'll go in there,</i>
<i>and I'd be playing</i>

95
00:06:21,631 --> 00:06:23,007
and I'd be closing my eyes, whatever,

96
00:06:23,091 --> 00:06:25,218
just practicing the same thing,
you know, and then I'd open,

97
00:06:25,301 --> 00:06:28,596
and there'd be, like, five elderly people

98
00:06:28,679 --> 00:06:31,599
just standing this close to me
just watching, sipping coffee,

99
00:06:31,682 --> 00:06:33,851
<i>thinking I was, like,</i>
<i>an attraction on the boat.</i>

100
00:06:34,394 --> 00:06:35,686
[chuckles]

101
00:06:36,187 --> 00:06:39,190
<i>I felt like this was the best way</i>
<i>to keep it private</i>

102
00:06:39,273 --> 00:06:40,942
<i>and not bug my neighbors.</i>

103
00:06:41,526 --> 00:06:43,277
[tapping]

104
00:06:45,738 --> 00:06:47,740
<i>I'd like to challenge myself to sound like</i>

105
00:06:47,824 --> 00:06:50,076
<i>I'm on a bigger drum kit</i>
<i>with two bass drums</i>

106
00:06:50,159 --> 00:06:51,619
<i>and a million cymbals.</i>

107
00:06:52,620 --> 00:06:54,622
<i>I always loved seeing drummers</i>

108
00:06:54,705 --> 00:06:57,250
<i>play really small kits</i>
<i>and just destroy them.</i>

109
00:07:04,132 --> 00:07:07,802
{\an8}<i>My favorite jazz drummer</i>
<i>has and always will be Buddy Rich.</i>

110
00:07:08,928 --> 00:07:12,890
{\an8}<i>He had crazy chops, and he was a showman.</i>

111
00:07:12,974 --> 00:07:16,102
{\an8}-[audience cheering, applauding]
<i>-He made the upside-down drum solo famous.</i>

112
00:07:16,644 --> 00:07:20,022
{\an8}<i>Most people think of his speed,</i>
<i>and how fun he was to watch.</i>

113
00:07:21,023 --> 00:07:24,986
{\an8}And ultimately, that's my two
favorite things about drummers.

114
00:07:28,739 --> 00:07:32,285
I would describe Travis's kit
as the Buddy Rich setup.

115
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:37,165
<i>One tom-tom in the front, two on the side,</i>

116
00:07:37,248 --> 00:07:40,626
<i>horizontal cymbals</i>
<i>that are flat, not tilted.</i>

117
00:07:41,127 --> 00:07:43,629
The smallest drum set, the most power.

118
00:07:43,713 --> 00:07:46,757
-[♪ upbeat electronic music playing]
<i>-♪ One, two, three ♪</i>

119
00:07:48,217 --> 00:07:50,011
[Stewart] <i>There's the monster kit guys,</i>

120
00:07:50,094 --> 00:07:52,805
and there's the guys
who can do it with less.

121
00:07:52,889 --> 00:07:55,600
♪♪

122
00:08:00,938 --> 00:08:04,358
[Questlove] <i>The one and last time</i>
<i>I did a drum battle with Trav,</i>

123
00:08:04,442 --> 00:08:06,068
he didn't let up for a moment.

124
00:08:07,612 --> 00:08:09,864
<i>And I was like,</i>
<i>"Don't let them see you give up.</i>

125
00:08:09,947 --> 00:08:13,159
You better do all these triple rolls."
[vocalizes triple rolls]

126
00:08:13,242 --> 00:08:16,287
[panting] And I'm like,
"You ready to stop yet? Fuck."

127
00:08:20,041 --> 00:08:23,252
[audience cheering]

128
00:08:23,336 --> 00:08:25,505
[Matt] <i>I mean, the guy's an artist</i>
<i>and he's a musician,</i>

129
00:08:25,588 --> 00:08:28,966
<i>but what he does is so athletic.</i>

130
00:08:30,009 --> 00:08:33,179
{\an8}The only thing akin to the way
that he plays drums I've seen

131
00:08:33,262 --> 00:08:35,681
{\an8}<i>is like a boxer, or a UFC fighter.</i>

132
00:08:36,849 --> 00:08:40,311
<i>You know, he goes into every show</i>
<i>like he's going into a ring.</i>

133
00:08:40,394 --> 00:08:41,938
[inaudible dialogue]

134
00:08:42,021 --> 00:08:44,023
[♪ dramatic classical music playing]

135
00:08:45,816 --> 00:08:47,568
[Lawrence] <i>Every time he hits a drum,</i>

136
00:08:47,652 --> 00:08:51,155
<i>there's a part of his anger</i>
<i>that comes out.</i>

137
00:08:52,573 --> 00:08:53,991
There's a lot of pain in Travis.

138
00:08:54,075 --> 00:08:57,286
[♪ dramatic classical music continues]

139
00:09:00,331 --> 00:09:03,834
[Mark Hoppus] <i>He is hitting</i>
<i>as hard as he possibly can,</i>

140
00:09:03,918 --> 00:09:05,795
{\an8}but it's never off time in any way.

141
00:09:08,506 --> 00:09:10,883
♪♪

142
00:09:10,967 --> 00:09:13,803
<i>Savage accuracy is the only way</i>
<i>I can describe it, I guess.</i>

143
00:09:16,472 --> 00:09:18,391
<i>♪♪</i>

144
00:09:18,474 --> 00:09:22,228
[Sheila E.] <i>When I first saw him play,</i>
<i>like, his high hat, it's so high.</i>

145
00:09:23,563 --> 00:09:25,356
<i>You want to reach</i>
<i>the people way out there,</i>

146
00:09:25,439 --> 00:09:28,192
{\an8}so y-you feel like you have to be big.

147
00:09:28,276 --> 00:09:29,360
{\an8}Bigger than life.

148
00:09:29,443 --> 00:09:31,779
♪♪

149
00:09:32,572 --> 00:09:35,491
<i>His elbow's gonna go out, his rotator.</i>

150
00:09:35,575 --> 00:09:38,536
<i>I'm thinking of all of the things</i>
<i>that are gonna hurt him later.</i>

151
00:09:42,707 --> 00:09:46,544
-My wrists, like, make lots of noises...
-[wrist clicking]

152
00:09:46,627 --> 00:09:47,920
...and they crack, and they pop.

153
00:09:49,171 --> 00:09:52,383
<i>There's a lot of blisters, a lot of cuts.</i>

154
00:09:53,467 --> 00:09:56,971
[Tim Armstrong]<i> One day, we're about</i>
<i>halfway through our set, Travis stops.</i>

155
00:09:57,805 --> 00:10:02,560
{\an8}I look closer, and his hand is opened up.

156
00:10:04,228 --> 00:10:05,730
<i>It was his bone.</i>

157
00:10:07,023 --> 00:10:08,649
[Daniel Jensen]
<i>He has this stuff called Dermabond,</i>

158
00:10:08,733 --> 00:10:12,695
{\an8}which is used for women
who have cesarean section births.

159
00:10:13,696 --> 00:10:16,490
[Tim]
<i>And he just puts the Dermabond on.</i>

160
00:10:17,158 --> 00:10:19,160
He sits up...

161
00:10:20,828 --> 00:10:22,121
"I'm good."

162
00:10:22,955 --> 00:10:25,833
<i>Finished the set, man,</i>
<i>like nothing ever happened,</i>

163
00:10:25,916 --> 00:10:27,376
but that's him.

164
00:10:28,002 --> 00:10:30,963
[♪ dramatic classical music continues]

165
00:10:33,549 --> 00:10:34,884
<i>Usually, you just watch the whole band,</i>

166
00:10:34,967 --> 00:10:36,927
<i>but I was directly zoomed in on him.</i>

167
00:10:38,971 --> 00:10:40,097
<i>This guy's an animal.</i>

168
00:10:41,057 --> 00:10:43,059
[Dr. Teeth] <i>♪ Be a better drummer,</i>
<i>be an up-and-comer ♪</i>

169
00:10:43,142 --> 00:10:44,602
<i>♪ Can you picture that? ♪</i>

170
00:10:45,645 --> 00:10:47,271
<i>♪ Can you picture that? ♪</i>

171
00:10:47,355 --> 00:10:48,856
Drum war!

172
00:10:48,939 --> 00:10:51,067
<i>♪♪</i>

173
00:10:53,819 --> 00:10:56,906
-Animal!
-[audience cheering]

174
00:10:56,989 --> 00:10:59,867
[Travis] <i>As a kid, the first thing</i>
<i>that got me interested in drums</i>

175
00:10:59,950 --> 00:11:01,952
<i>was Animal from The Muppets.</i>

176
00:11:05,289 --> 00:11:07,083
He was wild and always looked like

177
00:11:07,166 --> 00:11:09,377
he was having such a good time
behind the kit.

178
00:11:10,044 --> 00:11:12,755
[bellows, grunts]

179
00:11:12,838 --> 00:11:15,049
<i>Everyone else was just whatever.</i>

180
00:11:15,132 --> 00:11:17,301
<i>Animal was what I was drawn to.</i>

181
00:11:18,219 --> 00:11:21,180
<i>♪♪</i>

182
00:11:22,807 --> 00:11:24,058
Who's the Animal now?

183
00:11:24,392 --> 00:11:26,227
-Oh, yeah?
-[audience laughs]

184
00:11:26,310 --> 00:11:28,312
[grunts]

185
00:11:29,397 --> 00:11:30,815
[laughs]

186
00:11:30,898 --> 00:11:32,566
{\an8}He would go out in the kitchen,
pull pots and pans

187
00:11:32,650 --> 00:11:34,110
{\an8}out of the cupboard...

188
00:11:35,111 --> 00:11:38,197
{\an8}and he was banging on 'em,
and my wife, she said,

189
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:40,491
<i>"I think I'm gonna try</i>
<i>and make him a drummer."</i>

190
00:11:41,242 --> 00:11:43,327
At four, I-I got my first drum kit,

191
00:11:43,411 --> 00:11:45,871
and it was a Muppets drum kit.

192
00:11:45,955 --> 00:11:48,374
And he just beat the daylights out of it.

193
00:11:48,457 --> 00:11:51,252
-[♪ "death bed" by Powfu playing]
<i>-♪ Don't stay awake for too long... ♪</i>

194
00:11:51,335 --> 00:11:54,964
[Travis] <i>Well, as a kid,</i>
<i>I really didn't enjoy practicing.</i>

195
00:11:55,715 --> 00:11:57,675
At that age, you don't really want
to do anything.

196
00:11:57,758 --> 00:11:59,135
You just want to go play
with your friends.

197
00:11:59,218 --> 00:12:00,886
So, I'm really fortunate

198
00:12:00,970 --> 00:12:03,389
I had a really disciplined schedule
with practice.

199
00:12:03,472 --> 00:12:07,017
<i>♪ Don't stay awake for too long... ♪</i>

200
00:12:07,852 --> 00:12:10,521
[Tamara Barker] <i>My mom, she sat</i>
<i>in the room with him while he practiced.</i>

201
00:12:10,604 --> 00:12:12,314
<i>She would tape his drum lesson,</i>

202
00:12:12,398 --> 00:12:15,484
{\an8}so she knew exactly what it
was supposed to sound like

203
00:12:15,568 --> 00:12:17,027
{\an8}and what he was supposed to be doing.

204
00:12:19,071 --> 00:12:21,657
[Travis] <i>My mom learned</i>
<i>how to read music with me.</i>

205
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:24,827
<i>She could pretty much play</i>
<i>what I could play,</i>

206
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:27,496
<i>and she knew whether</i>
<i>I was practicing or not.</i>

207
00:12:27,580 --> 00:12:29,957
And she'd be like, "No, that's not right.
You're not doing that right."

208
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:34,503
<i>♪ Don't stay awake for too long... ♪</i>

209
00:12:34,587 --> 00:12:38,257
<i>She was adamant about me</i>
<i>just growing up to be something.</i>

210
00:12:39,216 --> 00:12:43,179
Like I was her little creation
that she was molding.

211
00:12:43,512 --> 00:12:46,557
<i>♪ I'm happy that you're here with me,</i>
<i>I'm sorry if I tear up... ♪</i>

212
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:48,726
[Randalai Barker]
<i>He was really close with my mom.</i>

213
00:12:48,809 --> 00:12:52,062
{\an8}Without my mom,
I don't think he would've practiced

214
00:12:52,146 --> 00:12:54,899
{\an8}and did all those things
to get where he is today.

215
00:12:54,982 --> 00:12:57,318
{\an8}<i>♪ ...every time you heard a bark</i>
<i>Cuddle... ♪</i>

216
00:12:57,401 --> 00:13:00,488
[Travis] <i>Growing up, you know,</i>
<i>I was, I was a mama's boy</i>

217
00:13:00,571 --> 00:13:03,657
<i>and Pops was there</i>
<i>to reinforce anything she said.</i>

218
00:13:05,034 --> 00:13:08,871
I was the enforcer.
She was the home teacher.

219
00:13:10,539 --> 00:13:12,041
[Travis]
<i>It was a very humble living.</i>

220
00:13:12,708 --> 00:13:15,836
<i>Pops built our house</i>
<i>with his own two hands.</i>

221
00:13:16,962 --> 00:13:19,465
<i>He was a Vietnam vet</i>
<i>and a steel mill worker</i>

222
00:13:19,548 --> 00:13:21,801
<i>that rode a Harley-Davidson every day.</i>

223
00:13:22,802 --> 00:13:25,471
<i>He was working 60 hours a week</i>
<i>to provide for us.</i>

224
00:13:25,930 --> 00:13:28,349
<i>So, I didn't get to spend</i>
<i>a whole lot of time with him.</i>

225
00:13:30,518 --> 00:13:33,938
[Randalai] <i>My dad never smiled.</i>
<i>He was very serious.</i>

226
00:13:34,522 --> 00:13:37,525
<i>Most of our friends were afraid of him</i>
<i>when they came to the house,</i>

227
00:13:37,608 --> 00:13:40,236
but I don't know anybody who didn't,
who didn't love our mom.

228
00:13:43,364 --> 00:13:45,115
[Randy Barker] <i>She was one fine lady.</i>

229
00:13:46,116 --> 00:13:48,035
Very dedicated to her kids.

230
00:13:50,830 --> 00:13:54,542
And then all of a sudden, the sides
of her face would swell up in here.

231
00:13:56,460 --> 00:14:00,631
For four months,
they treated her as having mumps.

232
00:14:01,841 --> 00:14:03,968
In the beginning,
they just didn't know what it was.

233
00:14:06,595 --> 00:14:09,557
<i>My mom was diagnosed</i>
<i>with Sjögren's syndrome,</i>

234
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:11,392
<i>which attacks the lymph nodes.</i>

235
00:14:13,018 --> 00:14:15,646
[Randy] <i>They told us</i>
<i>that she needed to have, uh,</i>

236
00:14:15,729 --> 00:14:17,523
<i>chemotherapy done right away.</i>

237
00:14:18,691 --> 00:14:20,359
<i>The next day, I went to work</i>

238
00:14:20,442 --> 00:14:23,195
and when I came home that night,
my daughter said, you know,

239
00:14:23,279 --> 00:14:24,655
"Something's the matter with Mom."

240
00:14:25,322 --> 00:14:26,407
Um...

241
00:14:28,659 --> 00:14:30,119
[clears throat]

242
00:14:31,245 --> 00:14:33,706
[♪ somber music playing]

243
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:39,962
Thirty days later, she was gone.

244
00:14:47,094 --> 00:14:49,930
[Travis] <i>I was confused, angry...</i>

245
00:14:51,140 --> 00:14:53,100
<i>kind of made me question God</i>

246
00:14:53,183 --> 00:14:56,478
<i>and just religion and everything</i>
<i>I'd been brought up on.</i>

247
00:14:57,730 --> 00:15:01,817
I was just like, "Wow, why would God
do something so fucked up?"

248
00:15:03,986 --> 00:15:06,155
<i>My pops wasn't really in a place</i>
<i>to answer anything</i>

249
00:15:06,238 --> 00:15:08,532
<i>because he was also grieving.</i>

250
00:15:10,576 --> 00:15:13,454
We didn't, uh, you know,
we really didn't discuss it

251
00:15:13,537 --> 00:15:16,248
that much, me and my, and my kids.

252
00:15:16,332 --> 00:15:18,208
We just kind of took it as it came.

253
00:15:21,170 --> 00:15:23,130
♪♪

254
00:15:23,213 --> 00:15:24,590
[Travis] <i>Before my mom passed,</i>

255
00:15:24,673 --> 00:15:28,010
<i>we found out my dad was in a relationship</i>
<i>with another woman...</i>

256
00:15:28,802 --> 00:15:30,804
<i>which made it even more difficult.</i>

257
00:15:33,015 --> 00:15:35,059
<i>He was spending a lot of time with her</i>

258
00:15:35,142 --> 00:15:37,645
<i>and then coming back</i>
<i>and staying with us when we needed him.</i>

259
00:15:38,479 --> 00:15:41,690
<i>I resented him for the obvious reasons.</i>

260
00:15:43,150 --> 00:15:46,153
<i>There wasn't, like,</i>
<i>that one stable foundation to go to</i>

261
00:15:46,236 --> 00:15:49,073
<i>and be like,</i>
<i>"Yo, this is difficult for me."</i>

262
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:55,996
<i>I just bottled up all my pain</i>
<i>and put it into drums.</i>

263
00:15:57,122 --> 00:15:59,708
<i>I think drums was my therapy.</i>

264
00:16:01,251 --> 00:16:04,546
<i>The last thing my mom said to me was,</i>

265
00:16:04,630 --> 00:16:08,300
<i>"Just focus on your music.</i>
<i>Become a drummer."</i>

266
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:12,137
<i>And I just came</i>
<i>on the first day of school,</i>

267
00:16:12,221 --> 00:16:14,223
<i>and I said, "I want in the marching band."</i>

268
00:16:15,766 --> 00:16:18,268
<i>It was drumline, jazz band.</i>

269
00:16:18,352 --> 00:16:19,812
<i>That was just my life.</i>

270
00:16:21,772 --> 00:16:25,109
How do I fulfill this goal
that my mom had for me?

271
00:16:25,192 --> 00:16:28,112
♪♪

272
00:16:28,195 --> 00:16:30,739
[tattoo machine buzzing]

273
00:16:35,953 --> 00:16:37,788
[Randalai]
<i>Travis was, overall, a good kid.</i>

274
00:16:38,539 --> 00:16:41,792
But after she passed away, he acted out.

275
00:16:43,836 --> 00:16:47,256
[Travis]
<i>I got my first tattoo when I was 15.</i>

276
00:16:47,965 --> 00:16:50,801
And my dad always said,
"The day you get a tattoo,

277
00:16:50,884 --> 00:16:53,262
I'm gonna kick your ass.
I'm gonna kick you out of the house."

278
00:16:53,929 --> 00:16:56,390
<i>So, I was kinda shook,</i>

279
00:16:56,473 --> 00:16:59,518
<i>but at the same time,</i>
<i>my mom had just passed away,</i>

280
00:16:59,601 --> 00:17:02,980
and in my head,
things couldn't get much worse.

281
00:17:03,063 --> 00:17:04,440
And I was rebellious.

282
00:17:04,523 --> 00:17:08,444
It was that age
where I just didn't... really give a fuck.

283
00:17:08,527 --> 00:17:10,904
[♪ hard rock music playing]

284
00:17:10,988 --> 00:17:13,282
[Randy] <i>I was really irritated.</i>

285
00:17:13,365 --> 00:17:15,701
I told him, "One day,
you're gonna be sorry,

286
00:17:15,784 --> 00:17:17,703
you know, that-that you did this."

287
00:17:19,163 --> 00:17:20,873
[Travis] <i>He'd be like, "You're an idiot.</i>

288
00:17:20,956 --> 00:17:23,208
You're never gonna be able to get a job."

289
00:17:23,292 --> 00:17:25,377
[Randy] <i>When he got out</i>
<i>of high school, I told him,</i>

290
00:17:25,461 --> 00:17:27,129
<i>"You better get yourself a job, boy."</i>

291
00:17:27,212 --> 00:17:29,590
And he said, "No, Dad,
I don't have to worry about getting a job.

292
00:17:29,673 --> 00:17:31,508
I'm-I'm gonna be in the music."

293
00:17:33,052 --> 00:17:36,263
I figured, if I get tattoos
all over my neck,

294
00:17:36,346 --> 00:17:38,223
and all over my body, all over my hands,

295
00:17:38,307 --> 00:17:41,852
I can't get a job,
and I won't have a plan B.

296
00:17:41,935 --> 00:17:43,520
And to me, that was perfect.

297
00:17:44,313 --> 00:17:47,149
<i>My goal was to make enough money to eat,</i>

298
00:17:47,232 --> 00:17:49,443
<i>have somewhere to sleep,</i>
<i>and play the drums.</i>

299
00:17:50,778 --> 00:17:52,446
My father gave me an ultimatum:

300
00:17:53,447 --> 00:17:56,825
<i>join the military,</i>
<i>work 60 hours a week and pay rent,</i>

301
00:17:56,909 --> 00:17:58,911
<i>or move out and do whatever you like.</i>

302
00:18:00,370 --> 00:18:02,873
<i>I told Pops, "I'm outta here.</i>
<i>I'm gonna go live with my friend</i>

303
00:18:02,956 --> 00:18:05,209
<i>"and play in a punk rock band,</i>

304
00:18:05,292 --> 00:18:08,337
and be a trash man in Laguna Beach."

305
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:09,630
And that's what I did.

306
00:18:10,297 --> 00:18:13,008
<i>♪ Follow me ♪</i>

307
00:18:15,260 --> 00:18:17,221
<i>When you work for the city</i>
<i>in Laguna Beach,</i>

308
00:18:17,304 --> 00:18:19,556
<i>if you're a trash man</i>
<i>and you work in downtown,</i>

309
00:18:19,640 --> 00:18:21,475
<i>they call you a "downtown rat."</i>

310
00:18:22,101 --> 00:18:23,727
So I was a downtown rat.

311
00:18:24,895 --> 00:18:26,814
[Taylor]
<i>Humble beginnings, I saw them.</i>

312
00:18:26,897 --> 00:18:28,649
<i>Trash man, literally.</i>

313
00:18:29,274 --> 00:18:31,860
Just trying to fucking
make something happen.

314
00:18:32,861 --> 00:18:35,572
{\an8}[♪ hard rock music playing]

315
00:18:39,326 --> 00:18:41,620
[Travis] <i>At the time, I was playing</i>
<i>in a band called Feeble,</i>

316
00:18:42,246 --> 00:18:44,414
and Feeble was playing around quite a bit.

317
00:18:44,498 --> 00:18:46,875
[♪ hard rock music continues]

318
00:18:48,502 --> 00:18:50,879
[Taylor] <i>I had a girlfriend at the time,</i>
<i>and she's like,</i>

319
00:18:50,963 --> 00:18:53,549
<i>"This band, Feeble, they have this kid."</i>

320
00:18:53,632 --> 00:18:55,384
She goes, "You gotta see
this kid play drums."

321
00:18:55,467 --> 00:18:58,095
♪♪

322
00:18:59,596 --> 00:19:01,557
[Noel Paris]
<i>A lot of people came just to watch him.</i>

323
00:19:02,266 --> 00:19:04,434
[singing indistinctly]

324
00:19:04,518 --> 00:19:07,563
{\an8}It was a sense of power
coming from this little, skinny kid

325
00:19:07,646 --> 00:19:09,314
{\an8}that I'd never seen before.

326
00:19:11,275 --> 00:19:13,068
[Taylor] <i>He was just a kid,</i>
<i>but he just had this, like,</i>

327
00:19:13,152 --> 00:19:16,113
<i>"Oh, you're gonna get picked up</i>
<i>by a band eventually</i>

328
00:19:16,196 --> 00:19:18,866
and you will be a star attraction
in that band."

329
00:19:18,949 --> 00:19:20,951
♪♪

330
00:19:22,536 --> 00:19:23,745
Fuckin' Travis.

331
00:19:23,829 --> 00:19:26,498
[cheering and applause]

332
00:19:27,708 --> 00:19:30,711
{\an8}[Christian Jacobs] <i>Our bass player came</i>
<i>to practice one night, and he was like,</i>

333
00:19:30,794 --> 00:19:33,297
<i>"Guys, I have the perfect kid.</i>
<i>He'll blow you away.</i>

334
00:19:33,380 --> 00:19:37,509
{\an8}"And he's only 18 or 19,
and he can go on tour.

335
00:19:37,593 --> 00:19:39,595
{\an8}And he's punk, he's perfect."

336
00:19:39,678 --> 00:19:42,472
[♪ ska music playing]

337
00:19:43,807 --> 00:19:45,559
{\an8}[Travis] <i>I know nothing about The Aquabats</i>

338
00:19:45,642 --> 00:19:47,728
{\an8}<i>except that I hear their name everywhere.</i>

339
00:19:47,811 --> 00:19:50,564
Like, everyone in our scene
was talking about them.

340
00:19:52,774 --> 00:19:54,151
<i>I show up the day of the show,</i>

341
00:19:54,818 --> 00:19:57,112
<i>and I know all the songs, I do soundcheck.</i>

342
00:19:57,613 --> 00:20:00,407
And he shows up, and he's like,
"Okay, what time we go on?"

343
00:20:00,490 --> 00:20:02,326
And I said, "Here,"
and I gave him his costume,

344
00:20:02,409 --> 00:20:05,454
and he was like, "W-Wait, what?"

345
00:20:09,583 --> 00:20:11,084
[Travis]
<i>I had all my friends come down,</i>

346
00:20:11,168 --> 00:20:13,629
and all my friends
were from Fontana or Riverside.

347
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:15,797
<i>They were all kinda hood.</i>

348
00:20:16,423 --> 00:20:19,509
<i>So they just thought it was</i>
<i>so funny I was up there</i>

349
00:20:19,593 --> 00:20:23,847
<i>in, like, a rashguard and a cape</i>
<i>and these fucking goggles,</i>

350
00:20:23,931 --> 00:20:26,183
<i>but there was two</i>
<i>to three thousand kids there.</i>

351
00:20:26,266 --> 00:20:27,643
<i>So I didn't care.</i>

352
00:20:27,726 --> 00:20:29,603
<i>-I was just excited to play.</i>
-[crowd cheering]

353
00:20:29,686 --> 00:20:31,980
You're the best kids in the world!

354
00:20:32,064 --> 00:20:35,108
[♪ "Super Rad!" by The Aquabats playing]

355
00:20:41,448 --> 00:20:45,035
[Bill Fold] <i>He gets into the band</i>
<i>and he was just a monster on the drums.</i>

356
00:20:45,118 --> 00:20:48,830
{\an8}I start paying attention to them,
and I end up managing them.

357
00:20:48,914 --> 00:20:51,083
<i>♪ We're on our way... ♪</i>

358
00:20:51,166 --> 00:20:53,001
<i>We put out </i>The Fury of the Aquabats!

359
00:20:53,085 --> 00:20:54,920
<i>on Goldenvoice Recordings.</i>

360
00:20:55,671 --> 00:21:00,259
<i>It went phenomenally well</i>
<i>for a ska band from another planet.</i>

361
00:21:01,802 --> 00:21:04,096
{\an8}<i>Travis moved into my house in Riverside.</i>

362
00:21:04,721 --> 00:21:08,475
{\an8}Travis was definitely the standout
because Travis was the punk rock kid.

363
00:21:09,101 --> 00:21:11,228
<i>So, like, it was always a problem</i>

364
00:21:11,311 --> 00:21:13,647
<i>with whether he'd wear the uniforms.</i>

365
00:21:13,730 --> 00:21:15,649
<i>♪ All systems go... ♪</i>

366
00:21:15,732 --> 00:21:19,111
[John Feldmann]
<i>I remember first time seeing Travis</i>

367
00:21:19,194 --> 00:21:22,781
<i>doing this marching cadence thing,</i>
<i>and I was like, </i>[laughing] <i>"Holy shit!"</i>

368
00:21:22,864 --> 00:21:24,992
{\an8}Because in our movement,
it was like everyone just did...

369
00:21:25,075 --> 00:21:26,326
{\an8}[vocalizes frantic drums]

370
00:21:26,410 --> 00:21:27,703
{\an8}or... [vocalizes rhythmic drums]

371
00:21:27,786 --> 00:21:29,871
{\an8}Those are, like, the two
kind of beats that you had.

372
00:21:31,415 --> 00:21:34,459
Our drummer,
I could just tell immediately was like,

373
00:21:34,543 --> 00:21:35,836
"This guy is a threat."

374
00:21:35,919 --> 00:21:38,046
<i>♪ All systems go ♪</i>

375
00:21:38,130 --> 00:21:41,466
<i>♪ As we step into the octagon ♪</i>

376
00:21:41,550 --> 00:21:44,136
<i>♪ Feel the wrath of mission codename ♪</i>

377
00:21:44,219 --> 00:21:46,179
<i>♪ Applesauce, Applesauce, Applesauce ♪</i>

378
00:21:46,263 --> 00:21:47,681
<i>♪ Super rad! ♪</i>

379
00:21:47,764 --> 00:21:51,310
[audience cheering]

380
00:21:51,393 --> 00:21:53,020
Right on, homies!

381
00:21:53,103 --> 00:21:55,314
[waves crashing]

382
00:21:56,189 --> 00:21:58,859
[♪ serene music playing]

383
00:22:00,277 --> 00:22:03,530
[Christian] <i>We didn't know a whole lot</i>
<i>about Travis's home life growing up.</i>

384
00:22:04,239 --> 00:22:06,658
Just little things that
he would tell us here and there.

385
00:22:08,952 --> 00:22:12,956
<i>But the one thing that</i>
<i>I always noticed was he was so driven.</i>

386
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:16,376
♪♪

387
00:22:20,672 --> 00:22:22,299
[Bill] <i>He is a walking metronome.</i>

388
00:22:23,633 --> 00:22:25,010
He's a perfectionist.

389
00:22:26,178 --> 00:22:29,264
[Lars Ulrich] <i>Guys that come out</i>
<i>of marching bands</i>

390
00:22:29,348 --> 00:22:31,933
<i>and really have the rudiments down,</i>

391
00:22:32,017 --> 00:22:34,936
{\an8}I admire it, uh, and I'm awestruck by it.

392
00:22:35,020 --> 00:22:36,980
♪♪

393
00:22:40,609 --> 00:22:42,444
[Questlove] <i>That's a lot of muscle memory.</i>

394
00:22:42,527 --> 00:22:46,448
That's a lot of discipline
and technique and commitment.

395
00:22:46,531 --> 00:22:49,076
♪♪

396
00:22:57,751 --> 00:23:00,128
[Christian]
<i>He'd always be on his practice pad.</i>

397
00:23:00,212 --> 00:23:02,923
<i>We'd be in the van,</i>
<i>like, traveling for hours,</i>

398
00:23:03,006 --> 00:23:06,343
and Travis would be on his headphones,
on his practice pad playing along.

399
00:23:07,886 --> 00:23:09,513
<i>He was always playing the drums.</i>

400
00:23:09,596 --> 00:23:12,099
[♪ drumming continues]

401
00:23:16,228 --> 00:23:18,647
<i>He was driven to get somewhere.</i>

402
00:23:19,648 --> 00:23:21,983
<i>He was trying to get away from something.</i>

403
00:23:23,610 --> 00:23:26,071
<i>I could just only piece things together.</i>

404
00:23:27,572 --> 00:23:29,658
<i>But I could tell he was kinda hurtin'.</i>

405
00:23:31,076 --> 00:23:33,495
<i>I think that his mom passing away</i>

406
00:23:33,578 --> 00:23:37,874
<i>was still really fresh in his heart</i>
<i>when he was in the band.</i>

407
00:23:39,167 --> 00:23:42,295
<i>♪♪</i>

408
00:23:46,299 --> 00:23:48,927
{\an8}[Mark] <i>We were playing a show</i>
<i>with The Aquabats,</i>

409
00:23:49,010 --> 00:23:51,638
{\an8}and our drummer had to leave.

410
00:23:52,264 --> 00:23:55,475
[Tom DeLonge] <i>Basically, there was</i>
<i>a family emergency with our old drummer.</i>

411
00:23:55,559 --> 00:23:57,894
<i>And actually, I believe</i>
<i>he was the one that says,</i>

412
00:23:57,978 --> 00:24:02,149
{\an8}"That cat from the opening band
probably can fill in. He's really good."

413
00:24:03,442 --> 00:24:06,027
[Travis] <i>Mark and Tom</i>
<i>come up to me and they said,</i>

414
00:24:06,111 --> 00:24:09,573
<i>"Yo, our drummer just left.</i>
<i>Would you consider playing?</i>

415
00:24:10,574 --> 00:24:13,743
The only-- The only catch is
the show starts in an hour."

416
00:24:15,328 --> 00:24:18,623
[Mark] <i>We grabbed our guitars,</i>
<i>we went into, uh, a dressing room,</i>

417
00:24:18,707 --> 00:24:21,042
uh, ran through everything
as best we could.

418
00:24:22,961 --> 00:24:24,379
[Travis] <i>You know, I didn't have</i>
<i>a drum kit backstage,</i>

419
00:24:24,463 --> 00:24:27,007
<i>but I just played on my knees...</i>

420
00:24:27,090 --> 00:24:29,759
and played our first show.

421
00:24:29,843 --> 00:24:31,887
[audience cheering]

422
00:24:31,970 --> 00:24:33,930
[Mark] <i>We're just moments away</i>
<i>from taking the stage</i>

423
00:24:34,014 --> 00:24:35,640
